%% Chapter 37: Bottom-Up Parsing
\label{ch:bottom-up-parsing}

\section{Introduction to Bottom-Up Parsing}
\label{sec:bottom-up-intro}

Bottom-up parsing constructs a parse tree from the leaves (input tokens) up to the root (start symbol). Rather than predicting which production to apply, the parser identifies the right-hand side of a production in the input and \emph{reduces} it to the left-hand side nonterminal. The most important class of bottom-up parsers is the LR family---LR(0), SLR(1), LALR(1), and canonical LR(1)---which can handle a strictly larger class of grammars than LL parsers.

The LR parsing theory was developed by Donald Knuth in his seminal 1965 paper ``On the Translation of Languages from Left to Right.'' Knuth showed that LR(k) grammars can be parsed efficiently and that the class of LR(k) languages is exactly the class of deterministic context-free languages.

\begin{historical}
Donald Knuth introduced LR parsing in 1965, proving that every LR(k) grammar can be parsed in time linear in the length of the input. The practical application of LR parsing was made feasible by the LALR(1) construction, developed by DeRemer (1969), which produces compact parsing tables. Frank DeRemer's thesis and subsequent work by Pager (1977) made LR parsing practical for compiler construction. The Yacc parser generator (Johnson, 1975) brought LR parsing to a wide audience. Modern tools like Bison (the GNU successor to Yacc) continue to use LALR(1) as the default parsing algorithm.
\end{historical}

\section{Shift-Reduce Parsing}
\label{sec:shift-reduce}

\subsection{Basic Shift-Reduce Parsing}
\label{sec:basic-sr}

A shift-reduce parser uses a stack and four actions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Shift}: Push the next input token onto the stack
  \item \textbf{Reduce}: Replace the top of the stack (matching a production's right-hand side) with the production's left-hand side nonterminal
  \item \textbf{Accept}: Declare the input successfully parsed
  \item \textbf{Error}: Report a syntax error
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Shift-Reduce Parsing]
\label{ex:shift-reduce}
Parse \texttt{id + id $\times$ id} using the expression grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Expression Grammar}
  E & \longrightarrow E + T \mid T \\
  T & \longrightarrow T \times F \mid F \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \mid \textbf{id}
\end{grammar}

\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
\textbf{Stack} & \textbf{Input} & \textbf{Action} \\
\hline
$\$$ & \texttt{id + id $\times$ id \$} & Shift \\
$\$ \texttt{id}$ & \texttt{+ id $\times$ id \$} & Reduce $F \to \texttt{id}$ \\
$\$ F$ & \texttt{+ id $\times$ id \$} & Reduce $T \to F$ \\
$\$ T$ & \texttt{+ id $\times$ id \$} & Reduce $E \to T$ \\
$\$ E$ & \texttt{+ id $\times$ id \$} & Shift \\
$\$ E +$ & \texttt{id $\times$ id \$} & Shift \\
$\$ E + \texttt{id}$ & \texttt{$\times$ id \$} & Reduce $F \to \texttt{id}$ \\
$\$ E + F$ & \texttt{$\times$ id \$} & Reduce $T \to F$ \\
$\$ E + T$ & \texttt{$\times$ id \$} & Shift \\
$\$ E + T \times$ & \texttt{id \$} & Shift \\
$\$ E + T \times \texttt{id}$ & \texttt{\$} & Reduce $F \to \texttt{id}$ \\
$\$ E + T \times F$ & \texttt{\$} & Reduce $T \to T \times F$ \\
$\$ E + T$ & \texttt{\$} & Reduce $E \to E + T$ \\
$\$ E$ & \texttt{\$} & Accept \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{example}

\subsection{Handles and Handle Pruning}
\label{sec:handles}

\begin{definition}[Handle]
\label{def:handle}
A \textbf{handle} of a right sentential form $\gamma = \alpha A w$ is a production $A \to \beta$ and a position in $\gamma$ where the substring $\beta$ occurs, such that replacing $\beta$ by $A$ produces the previous right sentential form in a rightmost derivation.
\end{definition}

The bottom-up parser works by repeatedly identifying and reducing handles---a process called \emph{handle pruning} that reverses a rightmost derivation.

\begin{theorem}[Handle Uniqueness]
\label{thm:handle-unique}
If a grammar is unambiguous, then every right sentential form has a unique handle.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Viable Prefixes}
\label{sec:viable-prefixes}

\begin{definition}[Viable Prefix]
\label{def:viable-prefix}
A \textbf{viable prefix} is a prefix of a right sentential form that does not extend past the right end of the rightmost handle. Equivalently, a viable prefix is a string that can appear on the stack of a shift-reduce parser.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Viable Prefixes are Regular]
\label{thm:viable-prefix-regular}
The set of viable prefixes of a CFG is a regular language.
\end{theorem}

This theorem is the foundation of LR parsing: the viable prefixes can be recognized by a finite automaton (the LR automaton), which forms the basis of the parsing tables.

\section{LR(0) Parsing}
\label{sec:lr0}

\subsection{LR(0) Items}
\label{sec:lr0-items}

\begin{definition}[LR(0) Item]
\label{def:lr0-item}
An \textbf{LR(0) item} (or \textbf{configuration}) for a grammar $G$ is a production $A \to \alpha \cdot \beta$ with a dot ($\cdot$) at some position in the right-hand side. The dot indicates how much of the production has been seen.
\end{definition}

For the production $A \to X Y Z$, the LR(0) items are:
\[
A \to \cdot X Y Z \qquad A \to X \cdot Y Z \qquad A \to X Y \cdot Z \qquad A \to X Y Z \cdot
\]

\subsection{The LR(0) Automaton}
\label{sec:lr0-automaton}

The LR(0) automaton is a DFA that recognizes viable prefixes. Its states are sets of LR(0) items (called \emph{item sets}), and its transitions are labeled by grammar symbols.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Construct the LR(0) automaton for grammar $G$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $I_0 \gets \texttt{closure}(\{S' \to \cdot S\})$ \Comment{augmented grammar}
\State Add $I_0$ to the set of states
\State $\texttt{worklist} \gets [I_0]$
\While{$\texttt{worklist}$ is not empty}
  \State Remove item set $I$ from $\texttt{worklist}$
  \For{each grammar symbol $X$}
    \State $J \gets \texttt{closure}(\texttt{goto}(I, X))$
    \If{$J \neq \emptyset$ and $J$ is not already a state}
      \State Add $J$ to the set of states
      \State Add $J$ to $\texttt{worklist}$
    \EndIf
    \If{$J \neq \emptyset$}
      \State Add transition $I \xrightarrow{X} J$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Closure and Goto}
\label{sec:closure-goto}

\begin{definition}[Closure]
\label{def:closure}
The \textbf{closure} of a set of items $I$ is computed by:
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $J \gets I$
\Repeat
  \For{each item $A \to \alpha \cdot B \beta$ in $J$}
    \For{each production $B \to \gamma$}
      \If{$B \to \cdot \gamma \notin J$}
        \State Add $B \to \cdot \gamma$ to $J$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\Until{$J$ does not change}
\State \textbf{return} $J$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Goto]
\label{def:goto}
$\texttt{goto}(I, X)$ is the closure of the set of all items $A \to \alpha X \cdot \beta$ such that $A \to \alpha \cdot X \beta$ is in $I$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{LR(0) Parsing Table Construction}
\label{sec:lr0-table}

The LR(0) parsing table has two parts:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{ACTION table}: $\texttt{ACTION}[i, a]$ specifies the action in state $i$ with lookahead $a$
    \begin{itemize}
      \item $\texttt{shift } j$ if $\texttt{goto}(I_i, a) = I_j$
      \item $\texttt{reduce } A \to \beta$ if $A \to \beta \cdot \in I_i$
      \item $\texttt{accept}$ if $S' \to S \cdot \in I_i$ and $a = \$$
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GOTO table}: $\texttt{GOTO}[i, A] = j$ if $\texttt{goto}(I_i, A) = I_j$
\end{itemize}

\section{SLR(1) Parsing}
\label{sec:slr1}

\subsection{The Problem with LR(0)}
\label{sec:lr0-problem}

LR(0) parsing is too weak for most practical grammars. The LR(0) automaton often contains states with both shift and reduce items, or multiple reduce items, creating \emph{conflicts}.

\begin{definition}[SLR(1) Improvement]
\label{def:slr1}
The \textbf{SLR(1)} (Simple LR) method resolves reduce--reduce and shift--reduce conflicts by using Follow sets: a reduce action $A \to \beta$ is taken in state $i$ only if the lookahead $a \in \follow{A}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[SLR(1) Parsing Table]
\label{def:slr1-table}
In the SLR(1) parsing table:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\texttt{ACTION}[i, a] = \texttt{reduce } A \to \beta$ if $A \to \beta \cdot \in I_i$ and $a \in \follow{A}$
  \item $\texttt{ACTION}[i, a] = \texttt{shift } j$ if $\texttt{goto}(I_i, a) = I_j$ (shift takes precedence over reduce)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{Canonical LR(1) Parsing}
\label{sec:canonical-lr1}

\subsection{LR(1) Items}
\label{sec:lr1-items}

\begin{definition}[LR(1) Item]
\label{def:lr1-item}
An \textbf{LR(1) item} is a pair $[A \to \alpha \cdot \beta, a]$ where $A \to \alpha \cdot \beta$ is an LR(0) item and $a$ is a \textbf{lookahead} terminal. The item means: we have seen $\alpha$ of the production $A \to \alpha \beta$, and we expect to see $a$ after the entire production is matched.
\end{definition}

\subsection{LR(1) Automaton Construction}
\label{sec:lr1-automaton}

The LR(1) automaton is constructed similarly to the LR(0) automaton, but using LR(1) items with lookahead propagation.

\begin{definition}[Closure for LR(1)]
\label{def:lr1-closure}
For LR(1) items, the closure operation propagates lookaheads:
If $[A \to \alpha \cdot B \beta, a]$ is in the closure, then for each production $B \to \gamma$, add $[B \to \cdot \gamma, b]$ for each $b \in \first{\beta a}$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Canonical LR(1) Parsing Tables}
\label{sec:canonical-lr1-tables}

The canonical LR(1) parsing table uses the exact lookahead from the LR(1) items, providing the most precise conflict resolution. However, the canonical LR(1) automaton can have many more states than the LR(0) automaton---often 2--3 times as many, and in the worst case exponentially more.

\section{LALR(1) Construction}
\label{sec:lalr1}

\subsection{The LALR(1) Idea}
\label{sec:lalr1-idea}

\begin{definition}[LALR(1)]
\label{def:lalr1}
\textbf{LALR(1)} (Lookahead LR) merges states of the canonical LR(1) automaton that have the same \emph{core} (same LR(0) items, ignoring lookaheads). The merged state has the union of the lookaheads.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[LALR(1) Properties]
\label{thm:lalr1-props}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The number of LALR(1) states equals the number of LR(0) states
  \item Every LALR(1) grammar is LR(1), but not vice versa
  \item Merging states can introduce reduce--reduce conflicts but never shift--reduce conflicts
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Constructing LALR(1) Tables}
\label{sec:lalr1-construction}

Two approaches to constructing LALR(1) tables:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Direct method}: Construct the LR(0) automaton and compute lookaheads directly using propagation and generation of lookaheads.
  \item \textbf{Merging method}: Construct the full LR(1) automaton and merge states with the same core.
\end{enumerate}

The direct method is more efficient in practice and is used by most parser generators.

\section{Conflicts and Their Resolution}
\label{sec:conflicts}

\subsection{Shift--Reduce Conflicts}
\label{sec:shift-reduce-conflicts}

A \textbf{shift--reduce conflict} occurs when a state contains both a shift item $A \to \alpha \cdot a \beta$ and a reduce item $B \to \gamma \cdot$ with the same lookahead $a$.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Resolution by default}: Shift (this resolves the ``dangling else'' problem correctly)
  \item \textbf{Resolution by precedence}: Assign precedence levels to operators and productions
  \item \textbf{Resolution by grammar rewriting}: Modify the grammar to eliminate the conflict
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Reduce--Reduce Conflicts}
\label{sec:reduce-reduce-conflicts}

A \textbf{reduce--reduce conflict} occurs when a state contains two reduce items $A \to \alpha \cdot$ and $B \to \beta \cdot$ with the same lookahead.

Reduce--reduce conflicts are more serious than shift--reduce conflicts and usually indicate a fundamental problem with the grammar. They are typically resolved by grammar rewriting.

\section{Yacc, Bison, and Modern LR Generators}
\label{sec:yacc-bison}

\subsection{Yacc}
\label{sec:yacc}

Yacc (Yet Another Compiler Compiler), written by Stephen Johnson at Bell Labs in 1975, was the first widely available LR parser generator. It reads an LALR(1) grammar specification and generates a C parser.

\begin{verbatim}
  %{
  #include <stdio.h>
  %}
  
  %token NUMBER PLUS TIMES LPAREN RPAREN
  
  %%
  
  expr: expr PLUS term    { printf("+"); }
      | term              ;
  term: term TIMES factor { printf("*"); }
      | factor            ;
  factor: LPAREN expr RPAREN
        | NUMBER          { printf("%d", $1); }
        ;
  
  %%
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Bison}
\label{sec:bison}

GNU Bison is a backward-compatible extension of Yacc that supports:
\begin{itemize}
  \item LALR(1), IELR(1), and GLR parsing
  \item Generalized LR parsing for ambiguous grammars
  \item Better error messages and conflict reporting
  \item Multiple target languages (C, C++, Java, D)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Modern LR Generators}
\label{sec:modern-lr}

Modern LR parser generators include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bison}: The standard for C/C++ parser generation
  \item \textbf{CUP}: LALR parser generator for Java
  \item \textbf{JavaCup}: Another Java LALR generator
  \item \textbf{Menhir}: An advanced LR(1) parser generator for OCaml
  \item \textbf{Lark}: An Earley/CYK parser for Python with LALR support
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:bu-summary}

Bottom-up parsing, particularly LR parsing, is the most powerful and widely used parsing technique for programming languages. Key concepts include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Shift-reduce parsing as the fundamental mechanism
  \item Handles and viable prefixes as the theoretical foundation
  \item LR(0), SLR(1), LALR(1), and canonical LR(1) as increasingly powerful variants
  \item Conflict detection and resolution for practical grammar design
  \item Parser generators (Yacc, Bison) that automate the construction of LR parsers
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The development of LR parsing from Knuth's theoretical foundation to practical parser generators is one of the great success stories of computer science. Key milestones include: Knuth's LR(k) theory (1965); DeRemer's LALR(1) construction (1969); Johnson's Yacc (1975); Pager's practical LALR(1) algorithm (1977); the GLR extension for ambiguous grammars (Tomita, 1984); and Bison's IELR(1) and GLR support (2000s). LR parsing remains the dominant parsing technique for production compilers.
\end{historical}

