\section{LALR(1) Construction: Detailed Treatment}
\label{sec:lalr1-detailed}

\subsection{Lookahead Computation by Propagation}
\label{sec:lookahead-propagation}

The direct method for computing LALR(1) lookaheads (due to DeRemer and Pennello, 1982) works by analyzing how lookaheads \emph{propagate} and are \emph{generated} during the closure and goto operations:

\begin{definition}[Lookahead Generation]
\label{def:lookahead-gen}
In the closure operation for LR(1) items, when we add $[B \to \cdot \gamma, b]$ because of $[A \to \alpha \cdot B \beta, a]$ with $b \in \first{\beta a}$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item If $b$ comes from $\first{\beta}$ (i.e., $b \in \first{\beta}$), then $b$ is \textbf{spontaneously generated} for $[B \to \cdot \gamma, b]$.
  \item If $b = a$ (i.e., $b$ comes from the lookahead of the triggering item), then the lookahead \textbf{propagates} from $[A \to \alpha \cdot B \beta, a]$ to $[B \to \cdot \gamma, a]$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Compute LALR(1) lookaheads by propagation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Construct the LR(0) automaton (item sets without lookaheads)
\State For each item in each state, initialize lookaheads to $\emptyset$
\State \textbf{Pass 1:} Compute spontaneous generations
\For{each state $I$ and each item $[A \to \alpha \cdot B \beta, \_]$ in $I$}
  \For{each production $B \to \gamma$}
    \For{each $b \in \first{\beta}$}
      \State Add $b$ to the spontaneous lookaheads of $[B \to \cdot \gamma]$ in $\text{goto}(I, B)$
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \textbf{Pass 2:} Compute propagation links
\For{each state $I$ and each item $i$ in $I$}
  \State Create a \emph{dummy} item $i'$ with a unique lookahead $\#$
  \State Compute $\text{closure}(\text{goto}(I, X))$ with $i'$
  \For{each item $j$ in $\text{goto}(I, X)$ that has lookahead $\#$}
    \State Record: lookaheads \emph{propagate} from $i$ to $j$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \textbf{Pass 3:} Propagate lookaheads to fixed point
\Repeat
  \State $\text{changed} \gets \text{false}$
  \For{each propagation link $i \to j$}
    \State $\text{new\_la} \gets \text{lookaheads}(j) \cup \text{lookaheads}(i)$
    \If{$\text{new\_la} \neq \text{lookaheads}(j)$}
      \State $\text{lookaheads}(j) \gets \text{new\_la}$
      \State $\text{changed} \gets \text{true}$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\Until{$\text{changed} = \text{false}$}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Direct LALR(1) Construction]
\label{thm:lalr1-complexity}
The direct LALR(1) construction runs in $O(n^2 \cdot |G|)$ time in the worst case, where $n$ is the number of LR(0) states and $|G|$ is the grammar size. In practice, it is much faster because the number of propagation links is typically small.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{IELR(1): An Improvement on LALR(1)}
\label{sec:ielr1}

\begin{definition}[IELR(1)]
\label{def:ielr1}
\textbf{IELR(1)} (Inadequacy Elimination LR) is a refinement of LALR(1) that splits states only when necessary to resolve \emph{inadequacies} (conflicts that arise from the merging of states with incompatible lookaheads).
\end{definition}

The IELR(1) algorithm:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start with the LALR(1) automaton.
  \item Identify states with conflicts (shift--reduce or reduce--reduce).
  \item For each conflict, determine whether it is a \emph{genuine} conflict (present in the canonical LR(1) automaton) or an \emph{artifact} of the LALR(1) merging.
  \item Split only those states whose conflicts are artifacts.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{theorem}[IELR(1) Properties]
\label{thm:ielr1-props}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item IELR(1) produces tables with at most as many states as canonical LR(1) and at least as many as LALR(1).
  \item IELR(1) resolves all conflicts that canonical LR(1) would resolve.
  \item In practice, IELR(1) tables are close in size to LALR(1) tables.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

GNU Bison uses IELR(1) as an alternative to LALR(1) when conflicts are detected.

\section{Conflict Resolution: Advanced Techniques}
\label{sec:conflict-resolution-advanced}

\subsection{Operator Precedence and Associativity}
\label{sec:precedence-associativity}

The most common source of shift--reduce conflicts is operator precedence. Parser generators resolve these using \emph{precedence declarations}:

\begin{verbatim}
  %left '+' '-'
  %left '*' '/'
  %right UMINUS
  
  %%
  expr: expr '+' expr    /* precedence of '+' */
      | expr '-' expr    /* precedence of '-' */
      | expr '*' expr    /* precedence of '*' */
      | expr '/' expr    /* precedence of '/' */
      | '-' expr %prec UMINUS  /* precedence of UMINUS */
      ;
\end{verbatim}

\begin{definition}[Precedence Resolution]
\label{def:prec-resolution}
When a shift--reduce conflict occurs between shifting token $a$ and reducing by production $p$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item If $\text{prec}(a) > \text{prec}(p)$: shift
  \item If $\text{prec}(a) < \text{prec}(p)$: reduce
  \item If $\text{prec}(a) = \text{prec}(p)$: use associativity
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Left-associative: reduce
      \item Right-associative: shift
      \item Non-associative: error
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Dangling Else Problem}
\label{sec:dangling-else}

The classic ``dangling else'' ambiguity is resolved by the default shift preference:

\begin{grammar}{Dangling Else}
  stmt & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } expr \textbf{ then } stmt \\
       & \mid \textbf{if } expr \textbf{ then } stmt \textbf{ else } stmt \\
       & \mid \text{other}
\end{grammar}

When the parser has parsed \texttt{if $E$ then if $E$ then $S$} and sees \texttt{else}, there is a shift--reduce conflict:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Shift}: The \texttt{else} belongs to the inner \texttt{if} (correct behavior)
  \item \textbf{Reduce}: The \texttt{else} belongs to the outer \texttt{if}
\end{itemize}
The default shift preference resolves this correctly, binding \texttt{else} to the nearest \texttt{if}.

\subsection{Grammar Rewriting to Eliminate Conflicts}
\label{sec:grammar-rewriting}

Some conflicts can be eliminated by restructuring the grammar:

\begin{example}[Eliminating Precedence Conflicts]
\label{ex:prec-rewrite}
The ambiguous expression grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Ambiguous Expressions}
  E & \longrightarrow E + E \mid E * E \mid ( E ) \mid \textbf{id}
\end{grammar}
can be rewritten to encode precedence:
\begin{grammar}{Precedence-Encoding Grammar}
  E & \longrightarrow E + T \mid T \\
  T & \longrightarrow T * F \mid F \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \mid \textbf{id}
\end{grammar}
The rewritten grammar has no conflicts because the structure enforces precedence.
\end{example}

\subsection{Conflict Resolution in Practice}
\label{sec:conflict-practice}

In practice, parser generators report conflicts and provide tools for resolution:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bison's \texttt{-v} flag}: Generates a \texttt{.output} file detailing all states and conflicts.
  \item \textbf{Bison's \texttt{\%expect} declaration}: Specifies the expected number of conflicts; the build fails if the count differs.
  \item \textbf{Menhir's conflict explanation}: Menhir can explain why a conflict occurs and suggest resolutions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
A small number of shift--reduce conflicts (typically 1--3) is normal in production grammars and is usually due to the dangling else pattern. Reduce--reduce conflicts are always a sign of a grammar error and should be eliminated.
\end{insight}

\section{GLR Parsing}
\label{sec:glr-parsing}

\subsection{Motivation for GLR}
\label{sec:glr-motivation}

LR parsers can only handle deterministic context-free languages. For ambiguous grammars or grammars that are not LR(k) for any $k$, a different approach is needed.

\begin{definition}[GLR Parsing]
\label{def:glr}
\textbf{GLR} (Generalized LR) parsing extends LR parsing to handle ambiguous and non-LR grammars. When a conflict occurs, the GLR parser forks into multiple parallel parse threads, each exploring a different resolution of the conflict.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The GLR Algorithm}
\label{sec:glr-algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{GLR parse of input $w$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Initialize a single parse thread with the LR(0) automaton
\State $\texttt{graph} \gets \text{empty graph}$ \Comment{Shared parse forest}
\For{each input token $a$}
  \For{each active parse thread $t$}
    \State $\text{action} \gets \text{ACTION}[t.\text{state}, a]$
    \If{action is \texttt{shift $j$}}
      \State Create new node in $\texttt{graph}$ for $a$
      \State Advance thread $t$ to state $j$
    \ElsIf{action is \texttt{reduce $A \to \beta$}}
      \State Create new node in $\texttt{graph}$ for $A$
      \State Thread $t$ continues with $A$ on the stack
    \ElsIf{action is a \textbf{conflict}}
      \State \textbf{Fork}: create a new thread for each alternative
    \ElsIf{action is \texttt{error}}
      \State Kill thread $t$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State Merge threads that are in the same state (sharing the parse forest)
\EndFor
\State \textbf{return} parse forest (if exactly one thread reached accept)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[GLR Complexity]
\label{thm:glr-complexity}
GLR parsing runs in $O(n^3)$ time for an ambiguous grammar (where $n$ is the input length) and $O(n)$ time for an unambiguous grammar that is LR(0). In general, GLR parsing is $O(n^3)$ for any CFG.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Parse Forests and Ambiguity Representation}
\label{sec:parse-forests}

When the input is ambiguous, the GLR parser produces a \emph{parse forest}---a compact representation of all parse trees:

\begin{definition}[Parse Forest]
\label{def:parse-forest}
A \textbf{parse forest} is a DAG (directed acyclic graph) where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each node represents a grammar symbol (terminal or nonterminal) at a specific position in the input.
  \item A node may have multiple children sets, representing alternative parse trees.
  \item The DAG structure shares common subtrees among different parse trees.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Parse Forest for Ambiguous Expression]
\label{ex:parse-forest}
The expression \texttt{1 + 2 * 3} parsed with an ambiguous grammar produces a forest with two trees:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Tree 1: $(1 + 2) * 3 = 9$
  \item Tree 2: $1 + (2 * 3) = 7$
\end{itemize}
The forest shares the leaf nodes (\texttt{1}, \texttt{+}, \texttt{2}, \texttt{*}, \texttt{3}) and branches at the root.
\end{example}

\subsection{Disambiguation in GLR}
\label{sec:glr-disambiguation}

After GLR parsing produces a parse forest, disambiguation filters out unwanted parse trees:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Precedence and associativity rules}: Filter trees that violate operator precedence.
  \item \textbf{Semantic predicates}: Evaluate semantic conditions on parse trees and reject those that fail.
  \item \textbf{Filter functions}: Custom functions that select the desired parse tree from the forest.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  /* Bison GLR disambiguation */
  %glr-parser
  %dprec 1  /* default precedence for rule 1 */
  %dprec 2  /* higher precedence for rule 2 */
\end{verbatim}

Bison's GLR mode supports \texttt{\%dprec} (dynamic precedence) and \texttt{\%merge} directives for disambiguating parse forests.
